Janis Papanagnou wrote:

> While I'd do that whenever I can it might turn out to be a problem
> with laptops; how many laptop's are out there that are able to run
> Linux? (Three [or so] years ago there was just one brand.) Laptops
> seem to require exotic device drivers, AFAIR, and there is/was no
> support from the manufacturers. Has that situation changed?

Short answer: Yes. 

Slightly longer answer: We have five laptops in the house, all running
Kubuntu Linux; both of the two-year-old Dells and the Thinkpad without
any trouble at all, the ancient Dell and even older Gateway with some
minor adjustments. The newer versions of most distributions run on many
laptops out of the box.

Don't hope for support from the manufacturers, though; I was once told by
a Dell tech support critter, when I called it for a hardware problem,
that it was my own fault for not running Windows.

The Linux community, however, is a better bet. Some useful links:
www.linux-laptop.net
www.linux.org/hardware/laptop.html
www.tuxmobil.org (also for running Linux on PDAs)

